tors of a 3x3 Matrix With Distinct Eigenvalues" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129325 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186129963 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129325 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050820 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129325 #edb/ref 1050 1020.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129325 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "0e238e7b-3085-5b87-b3c4-0f5c35258e51" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129963 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186129964 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129963 #edb/ref 1061 20099 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129963 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "875dc5aa-e4f4-50f8-a30e-d5ed46d77e32" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129963 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186130347 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129964 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186129965 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129964 #edb/ref 1061 20100 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129964 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "59537f37-7060-5544-9b4a-84501e450c50" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129964 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186129127 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129965 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186130084 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129965 #edb/ref 1061 20101 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129965 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "8a926ac2-afd2-5b57-8911-c3b8dac86d39" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129965 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186129128 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129966 #edb/ref 1061 20102 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129966 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "5d1fcdf4-d676-5ff5-bf31-d7e371f43f08" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129966 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186129129 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130084 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186129966 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130084 #edb/ref 1061 27265 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130084 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3314b023-f15c-5f8b-a4e4-aeea702377a2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130084 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186130403 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130347 #edb/ref 1064 "Consider the following $3\\times 3$ matrix: \n\n![](images/4f/4fd591d8028847aa7e1d11e840acdb192703d152f9c397e40fec98fd1e281c41.svg)\n\nIf we are told that one of the eigenvalues of this matrix is $\\lambda = 3,$ how do we calculate the corresponding eigenvector?\n\nTo compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation\n\n$$\n(A - \\lambda I) \\textbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =3.$ So, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 6 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -4\\end{bmatrix}-3\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 6 & -8 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -7\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, to find the eigenvectors, we have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}6{x}_{2}-8{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ -3{x}_{2}+6{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ 3{x}_{2}-7{x}_{3} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSo, we row-reduce the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 6 & -8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -7 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-\\frac{1}{2}){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 6 & -8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+\\frac{3}{2}{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 6 & -8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this reduced matrix, there are two pivot columns (the $2$nd and $3$rd). Thus, $x_1$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we have $x_3 = 0.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain $x_2 = 0.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n\n![](images/70/705dd92c7330897f35671b66866791160b9d7f65e3280d5ccb9d992405c08942.svg)\n\nSetting $x_1=1,$ we get the following eigenvector:\n\n![](images/2a/2a5dd6c35fb81134f13e765e0a02be4ea2feedfd878a1923491813492739ef00.svg)" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186130347 #edb/ref 1065 3566 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130347 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "b1a9d148-15f7-5878-8f36-f19ee99c49d6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130347 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130403 #edb/ref 1064 "Note the following important theorem.\n\n*The set of eigenvectors $\\{ \\mathbf{v}_1,\\mathbf{v}_2, \\ldots, \\mathbf{v}_k \\}$ of a matrix $A,$ where each vector $\\mathbf v_i$ corresponds to a different eigenvalue, is linearly independent.*\n\n**Watch out!** Eigenvectors that correspond to the *same* eigenvalue *can* be (and very often *will* be) linearly dependent." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130403 #edb/ref 1065 4432 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130403 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "c0f0f6d3-c9e1-52a6-bf51-244bb7751a9d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130403 #edb/ref 1067 "Eigenvectors That Correspond to Different Eigenvalues" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269730 #edb/ref 1080 "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186269730 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269730 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "3279f5ec-b5ad-574c-aebf-693bd2fdaa1c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186270171 #edb/ref 1080 "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186270171 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186270171 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "653c963e-8cbf-558c-a076-0707a4bd7a0e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186270375 #edb/ref 1080 "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186270375 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186270375 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "7e7e2990-b7e7-5be9-a37d-ee55358620c1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186270392 #edb/ref 1080 "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186270392 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186270392 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "81fcddb5-5383-56b8-bcf4-c993974f8e45" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271045 #edb/ref 1080 "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186271045 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271045 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "ca767cd0-e2d5-5f15-b839-31ed41909f67" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271748 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186269730 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271748 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186270171 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271748 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186270375 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271748 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186270392 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271748 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186271045 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271748 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271748 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271748 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186270392 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271748 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "87f091c3-7851-5215-bc6b-9b792d0722c2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272147 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271748 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272147 #edb/ref 1069 "q-140717" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272147 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272147 #edb/ref 1071 "Find an eigenvector of $A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 3 & -3 & -3\\end{bmatrix}$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186272147 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "00737dc0-f397-573a-80e1-d038ce87150d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272147 #edb/ref 1074 "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =3.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 3 & -3 & -3\\end{bmatrix}-2\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -3 & -2 \\\\ 3 & -3 & -5\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvectors corresponding to this eigenvalue, we have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2}-5{x}_{3} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSo, we row-reduce the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -3 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -3 & -5 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:=(-1){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -3 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -3 & -5 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}-3{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}-3{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}-(\\frac{1}{4}){R}_{2} \\\\ {R}_{2}:=(\\frac{1}{4}){R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns (the $1$st and the $3$rd). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain ${x}_{3}=0.$ Substituting this in the first equation, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{2}+2{x}_{3} \\\\  & ={x}_{2}+2(0) \\\\  & ={x}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector\n$v=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186321179 #edb/ref 1080 "-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186321179 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186321179 #edb