hat correspond to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=1$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2$ of the matrix $A,$ given above. Find the third eigenvector ${v}_{3}=[a,\\,b,\\,c]^{T}$ such that the set $\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\}$ is linearly independent. What is the value of $\\frac{a}{b}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186323913 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b75fe299-3a00-56ce-ac2b-3a6b8472e075" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323913 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we find all the eigenvalues of $A$ by solving the characteristic equation $det(A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}-1-\\lambda  & 3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4-\\lambda  & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )\\begin{vmatrix}-1-\\lambda  & 3 \\\\ 2 & 4-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )((-1-\\lambda )(4-\\lambda )-(3)(2)) & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}-3\\lambda -10) & =0 \\\\ -(\\lambda -1)(\\lambda +2)(\\lambda -5) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, ${\\lambda }_{1}=1,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=5.$ Since we obtained three distinct eigenvalues, the set of eigenvectors $\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\},$ where each vector corresponds to different eigenvalue, will be linearly independent.\n\nNow, recall that ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ correspond to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=1$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2.$ Thus, the third eigenvector ${v}_{3}$ must correspond to ${\\lambda }_{3}=5.$ \n\nTo compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =5.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}-5\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvectors corresponding to this eigenvalue, we have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-6{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ -4{x}_{3} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSo, we row-reduce the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\frac{5}{3} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-\\frac{12}{5}){R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\frac{5}{3} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns (the $1$st and the $3$rd). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain ${x}_{3}=0.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we get ${x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2}.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=2,$ we get the following eigenvector:\n\n$$\nv=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{1}{2}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186355007 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{1}{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186355007 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186355007 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "290f4389-144e-58ae-8aa0-f8779961574c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186355251 #edb/ref 1080 "2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186355251 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186355251 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "426fd857-13b1-5ab3-9758-9722e35fc330" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186355266 #edb/ref 1080 "-2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186355266 #edb/ref 1082 #edb