 value of $\\frac{a}{b}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186381111 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c980d806-f256-5388-b798-65b34624c78e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381111 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we find all the eigenvalues of $A$ by solving the characteristic equation $det(A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}3-\\lambda  & 0 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 3-\\lambda  & -1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 3-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (3-\\lambda )\\begin{vmatrix}3-\\lambda  & -1 \\\\ -1 & 3-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (3-\\lambda )[(3-\\lambda )^{2}-1] & =0 \\\\ (3-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}-6\\lambda +8) & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -3)(\\lambda -4)(\\lambda -2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, ${\\lambda }_{1}=3,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=4,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=2.$ Since we obtained three distinct eigenvalues, the set of eigenvectors $\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\},$ where each vector corresponds to different eigenvalue, will be linearly independent.\n\nNow, recall that ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ correspond to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=3$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=4.$ Thus, the third eigenvector ${v}_{3}$ must correspond to ${\\lambda }_{3}=2.$ \n\nTo compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =2.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}-(2)\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvectors corresponding to this eigenvalue, we have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-3{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}-{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ -{x}_{2}+{x}_{3} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSo, we row-reduce the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{2}+{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns (the $1$st and the $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain ${x}_{2}={x}_{3}.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we get ${x}_{1}=3{x}_{3}.$\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}3{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{3}=1,$ we get the following eigenvector:\n\n$$\nv=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{3}{1}=3.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186381112 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186381113 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381112 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186381114 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381112 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186381115 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381112 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186381116 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381112 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186381117 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381112 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381112 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381112 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186381113 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381112 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "b84bb508-82f8-5a30-b1a6-92bf25dfe082" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381113 #edb/ref 1080 "3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381113 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381113 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "2118b9d8-3a94-5241-ac9a-03c1a8165254" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381114 #edb/ref 1080 "2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381114 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186